1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,450
1775. Paul Revere rides. "The British are coming!"

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:07,950
Nope. He never shouted that.

3
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:10,417
Paul Revere: the midnight ride myth.

4
00:00:10,567 --> 00:00:13,317
The ride was a secret mission.

5
00:00:13,467 --> 00:00:16,217
British patrols were everywhere.

6
00:00:16,367 --> 00:00:18,750
Shouting would get him caught.

7
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:21,850
Also, everyone there was British.

8
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,283
So yelling "the British" made no sense.

9
00:00:25,433 --> 00:00:29,250
He reportedly said: "The Regulars are coming out."

10
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:32,483
Regulars meant British soldiers.

11
00:00:32,633 --> 00:00:34,683
And he wasn't alone.

12
00:00:34,833 --> 00:00:37,483
William Dawes rode too.

13
00:00:37,633 --> 00:00:41,450
Revere got captured on the way to Concord.

14
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:45,017
A third rider, Samuel Prescott, made it.

15
00:00:45,167 --> 00:00:48,250
So why is Revere the famous one?

16
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:51,083
A poem. 1861.

17
00:00:51,233 --> 00:00:54,850
The poet Longfellow made Revere the hero.

18
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,950
The other two riders? Left out.

19
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:01,850
Times he shouted "The British are coming": zero.

20
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,317
Anyway, here's Paul Revere shouting.
